id:"matters", name:"Matters.AI", origin:"India &#183; Bengaluru, founded 2024", kind:"Direct", tag:"Verdict first, then the evidence",
thesis:"The product the brief is about. The alert opens on a verdict the AI has already reached and the remediation it has already run, and everything below that is the case for the verdict, in four named evidence sections. The analyst is reviewing a decision, not making one.",
users:[
 "Left navigation groups the product by object: RISKS (Misconfigurations, Alerts, Exposure, DAM), DATA INVENTORY (Cloud, SaaS, Data Lineage, Endpoint), DATA CATALOGUE (Data Elements), then Project Settings. Alerts is one item among risks, not the home screen.",
 "Two human fields on the list, Status and Analyst Verdict, so the product expects a person to record a judgment separately from where the work is.",
 "Activity &amp; Comments at the foot of the alert logs who changed the analyst verdict and when, with a comment box and a Publish button, so the record is written by people as well as by the AI.",
 "Not established: roles, permissions, who may run Remediate with MattersAI, and whether the analyst can overrule the AI verdict without a comment."],
scale:{claim:"Thousands. The list reads Showing 1 to 10 of 11,487 across 1,149 pages, in a demo project.",
 evidence:["Footer of the alerts list: <code>Rows Per Page 10</code>, <code>Showing 1 - 10 of 11487</code>, pagination to page <code>1149</code>.",
 "Twelve columns, a Search box, Group By and Add Filter above the table, and a Refresh button. Severity is the second column.",
 "Most visible rows are Low and Medium AWS S3 detections; one Critical sits at the top. The demo queue is not sorted by severity by default."],
 inference:"11,487 is a demo tenant count, so it says what volume Matters chose to show, not a customer average. Ten rows per page against eleven thousand alerts means the list is not meant to be read down; it is meant to be filtered and grouped, and the Group By control is placed accordingly. Inference, not a published figure."},
approach:["The verdict is the headline. The alert title itself ends in BLOCKED, and the first block under it is an Alert Verdict reading <em>True Positive, Malicious Data Exfiltration Detected and Blocked by Matters AI</em>, in a red-tinted box.",
 "Remediation is reported as already done. Automated Remediation Completed lists three actions with ticks: the user locked from file upload on the company laptop, a fingerprint and confidential tag applied to the file, and a prevention policy created and deployed. A green line closes it: all actions successfully executed.",
 "Evidence is structured under one heading, How Matters AI Reached This Verdict, with four collapsible sections in a fixed order: Classification, Data Lineage, User Behaviour, Predictive Modelling. Each has a one-line summary visible while collapsed.",
 "Every evidence section ends in a Why High Risk? list, so the reasoning is written as bullet points rather than as a score.",
 "Data Lineage is a chain of rows, each labelled Normal or Risky, with person and role, action, file and destination, ending at an external Gmail address with a callout of what the system detected.",
 "User Behaviour compares the user against a bubble chart of what their department normally touches, and Predictive Modelling adds a Model Insight, here <em>On notice period</em>, as context for the risk.",
 "The actions bar is small and fixed: Remediate with MattersAI, Create Ticket, an assignee dropdown, a Muted dropdown, and View Policy next to the severity pill. A separate Ask Matters AI button sits on the summary.",
 "A right rail holds Alert Details (ID, detected time, account, datastore, datastore type, scope name, database user) and Detection Type as chips: Data Exfiltration, Policy Violation, Insider Threat, Anomalous Sharing."],
